1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,152
Sam's fast first draft is the mistake this lecture exists to prevent, and it's worth

2
00:00:05,152 --> 00:00:06,924
knowing before you start yours.

3
00:00:06,924 --> 00:00:11,669
The angle map is the document that turns twenty from a number in Section one into a

4
00:00:11,669 --> 00:00:16,356
real list of twenty genuinely different bets, not twenty confident restatements of

5
00:00:16,356 --> 00:00:18,242
the first idea that came to mind.

6
00:00:18,242 --> 00:00:22,987
Pull up the product truth doc and the hook matrix from the last two lectures if you

7
00:00:22,987 --> 00:00:26,874
have them, because this works better done alongside me than watched.

8
00:00:26,874 --> 00:00:29,847
If you don't have them open, pause and get them now.

9
00:00:29,847 --> 00:00:34,477
What follows only sticks if you're actually filling in rows as we go, not nodding

10
00:00:34,477 --> 00:00:36,421
along and planning to do it later.

11
00:00:36,421 --> 00:00:40,937
Later rarely happens, and a half-built angle map is worse than none, because it

12
00:00:40,937 --> 00:00:44,824
looks finished without being useful: the exact trap Sam walked into.

13
00:00:45,374 --> 00:00:50,184
Here's the actual method, and it's less mysterious than "twenty testable angles"

14
00:00:50,184 --> 00:00:50,604
sounds.

15
00:00:50,604 --> 00:00:55,234
For each of the five awareness rungs, you ask three questions: what does this

16
00:00:55,234 --> 00:00:59,863
specific person feel right now, which hook pattern from last lecture's matrix

17
00:00:59,863 --> 00:01:04,432
actually fits that feeling, and which one proof point from the product truth

18
00:01:04,432 --> 00:01:05,694
document backs it up.

19
00:01:05,694 --> 00:01:10,444
Answer that twice per rung, honestly, in two genuinely different ways, and five

20
00:01:10,444 --> 00:01:13,991
rungs times two angles times two passes gets you to twenty.

21
00:01:13,991 --> 00:01:19,041
Notice the arithmetic is doing something specific: it forces you through every rung,

22
00:01:19,041 --> 00:01:24,031
every time, rather than letting you write ten angles for the rung that's easiest to

23
00:01:24,031 --> 00:01:25,834
write for and calling it done.

24
00:01:25,834 --> 00:01:28,840
That discipline is the entire value of the method.

25
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:33,469
Anyone can generate twenty variants on the one feeling they thought of first.

26
00:01:33,469 --> 00:01:36,295
It's also why Sam's eleven-minute draft failed.

27
00:01:36,295 --> 00:01:41,225
He answered the three questions once, fast, then reworded the same answer nineteen

28
00:01:41,225 --> 00:01:45,673
more times instead of going back through the rungs a genuinely second way.

29
00:01:45,673 --> 00:01:50,723
The method only works if the second pass is honestly different from the first, not a

30
00:01:50,723 --> 00:01:51,986
faster version of it.

31
00:01:52,536 --> 00:01:57,702
Here are six of the twenty, and notice they're not six versions of one idea wearing

32
00:01:57,702 --> 00:01:58,822
different outfits.

33
00:01:58,822 --> 00:02:04,050
Hour eight and twelve-hour shift both sit low on the ladder, but interrupt different

34
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:08,904
feelings: one is physical, one is a creeping normalisation the audience hasn't

35
00:02:08,904 --> 00:02:09,838
questioned yet.

36
00:02:09,838 --> 00:02:14,506
The 15-20 spec and not-just-for-flights sit on the same rung and answer two

37
00:02:14,506 --> 00:02:16,248
completely different doubts.

38
00:02:16,248 --> 00:02:21,352
That's the actual test for whether your twenty angles are real: could you swap two

39
00:02:21,352 --> 00:02:25,086
of them and lose something specific, or would nobody notice?

40
00:02:25,086 --> 00:02:28,073
Run that test on the two product-aware rows too.

41
00:02:28,073 --> 00:02:31,559
"A nurse's honest review" is answering a trust question.

42
00:02:31,559 --> 00:02:34,733
"Cost of a bad pair" is answering a value question.

43
00:02:34,733 --> 00:02:39,774
Someone hesitating over Harlan for either reason exists, and they're not the same

44
00:02:39,774 --> 00:02:44,939
person, which is exactly why one row for "product-aware, but persuasive" would have

45
00:02:44,939 --> 00:02:47,180
quietly served neither of them well.

46
00:02:47,730 --> 00:02:52,404
Remember the warning from the volume maths lecture: twenty near-identical variants

47
00:02:52,404 --> 00:02:54,855
don't behave like twenty independent shots.

48
00:02:54,855 --> 00:02:57,192
This is where that warning gets concrete.

49
00:02:57,192 --> 00:03:00,213
A reskin swaps an adjective and calls it a new angle.

50
00:03:00,213 --> 00:03:04,773
A real angle answers a different question entirely, even if it lands on the same

51
00:03:04,773 --> 00:03:05,913
rung as another one.

52
00:03:05,913 --> 00:03:10,644
If you can't say, in one sentence, what specific doubt or feeling an angle is aimed

53
00:03:10,644 --> 00:03:13,381
at that the others aren't, it's not a new angle.

54
00:03:13,381 --> 00:03:17,941
It's a reskin wearing a different hook, exactly what Sam found buried in his own

55
00:03:17,941 --> 00:03:20,278
first draft at the start of this lecture.

56
00:03:20,278 --> 00:03:24,781
Here's a quick check you can run on your own map once it's built: read just the

57
00:03:24,781 --> 00:03:29,455
middle column, the core feeling each row interrupts, top to bottom, with the angle

58
00:03:29,455 --> 00:03:30,253
names covered.

59
00:03:30,253 --> 00:03:34,927
If two rows read as the same sentence with different words, you've found a reskin,

60
00:03:34,927 --> 00:03:38,860
and it's worth replacing before you generate a single static from it.

61
00:03:39,410 --> 00:03:43,165
One honest limit before you treat this map as finished work.

62
00:03:43,165 --> 00:03:48,359
Building a real angle map is real craft, and it's tempting to feel done once you've

63
00:03:48,359 --> 00:03:50,799
hit twenty rows that all look distinct.

64
00:03:50,799 --> 00:03:52,802
Distinct isn't the same as good.

65
00:03:52,802 --> 00:03:57,182
Some of these twenty will be flatly wrong for Harlan, and that's fine.

66
00:03:57,182 --> 00:04:00,124
The map's job is coverage, not quality control.

67
00:04:00,124 --> 00:04:05,130
Quality control starts in Section six, and the test in Section seven is the only

68
00:04:05,130 --> 00:04:09,886
thing that actually tells you which of these twenty was worth the afternoon.

69
00:04:09,886 --> 00:04:14,830
If that feels like a lot of trust to place in twenty rows on a page, that's the

70
00:04:14,830 --> 00:04:15,768
right instinct.

71
00:04:15,768 --> 00:04:20,962
It is a lot of trust, and it's exactly the amount the maths in Section one said was

72
00:04:20,962 --> 00:04:21,651
reasonable.

73
00:04:21,651 --> 00:04:25,030
Not more, chasing certainty the method can't give you.

74
00:04:25,030 --> 00:04:30,224
Not less, and quietly recreating the four-variant problem this whole section exists

75
00:04:30,224 --> 00:04:30,662
to fix.

76
00:04:31,212 --> 00:04:35,972
Next lecture closes a gap that kills otherwise-winning ads: the angle can be

77
00:04:35,972 --> 00:04:41,170
perfect, and the ad can convert the scroll, and it still loses the sale if the page

78
00:04:41,170 --> 00:04:43,487
it lands on doesn't keep the promise.

79
00:04:43,487 --> 00:04:46,806
Before that, do the check Sam wishes he'd done first.

80
00:04:46,806 --> 00:04:52,005
Read your own twenty rows the way he eventually did: cover the angle names and read

81
00:04:52,005 --> 00:04:54,385
just the middle column, top to bottom.

82
00:04:54,385 --> 00:04:59,520
If two rows sound like the same sentence with different words, fix that now, while

83
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:03,340
it costs nothing, not after you've generated statics from it.

84
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:08,413
Whatever survives that pass is the angle map that becomes part of artifact B01 in

85
00:05:08,413 --> 00:05:09,478
the 2.6 workshop.
